format 1
adapter curriculum 1
unit: radioactivity

objective u2-recognise-radiation

statement:
    Say what each one is: alpha is 2 protons and 2 neutrons, beta is a fast electron, gamma is high-energy electromagnetic radiation.

misconception: The electron of a beta particle was orbiting the nucleus.

item u2-l2-choice-alpha
type: choice
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radioactive-decay

prompt:
    Which kind of radiation is made of 2 protons and 2 neutrons?

options:
    {"id":"alpha","label":"Alpha"}
    {"id":"beta","label":"Beta","note":"A beta particle is a fast electron."}
    {"id":"gamma","label":"Gamma","note":"Gamma is electromagnetic radiation. It is not made of protons and neutrons."}

answer: alpha

explanation:
    An alpha particle is 2 protons and 2 neutrons stuck together, the nucleus of a helium atom.

item u2-l2-choice-electron
type: choice
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radioactive-decay

prompt:
    A fast electron is released from a nucleus. What is this radiation called?

options:
    {"id":"beta","label":"Beta"}
    {"id":"alpha","label":"Alpha","note":"Alpha is 2 protons and 2 neutrons, not an electron."}
    {"id":"gamma","label":"Gamma","note":"Gamma is electromagnetic radiation, not a particle."}

answer: beta

explanation:
    A fast electron released from a nucleus is a beta particle.

item u2-l2-match-kinds
type: match
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radioactive-decay

prompt:
    Match each kind of radiation to what it is.

pairs:
    {"id":"alpha","left":"Alpha","right":"2 protons and 2 neutrons"}
    {"id":"beta","left":"Beta","right":"A fast electron"}
    {"id":"gamma","left":"Gamma","right":"High-energy electromagnetic radiation"}

explanation:
    Alpha is 2 protons and 2 neutrons. Beta is a fast electron. Gamma is high-energy electromagnetic radiation.

item u2-l2-predict-release
type: predict
surfaces: lesson

prompt:
    When a radioactive nucleus changes, what can it release?

input: choice

options:
    {"id":"particle","label":"Only a particle"}
    {"id":"energy","label":"Only energy"}
    {"id":"both","label":"A particle, energy, or both"}
    {"id":"nothing","label":"Nothing at all"}

answer: both

explanation:
    Your guess is not scored. The answer comes later in the lesson.

item u2-x-em-burst
type: choice
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radioactive-decay

prompt:
    A burst of high-energy electromagnetic radiation leaves a nucleus. What is it?

options:
    {"id":"gamma","label":"Gamma"}
    {"id":"alpha","label":"Alpha","note":"Alpha is a particle of 2 protons and 2 neutrons."}
    {"id":"beta","label":"Beta","note":"Beta is a fast electron."}

answer: gamma

explanation:
    High-energy electromagnetic radiation from a nucleus is gamma radiation.

item u2-x-helium-nucleus
type: choice
difficulty: 2
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radioactive-decay

prompt:
    Which kind of radiation is the same as the nucleus of a helium atom?

options:
    {"id":"alpha","label":"Alpha"}
    {"id":"beta","label":"Beta","note":"Beta is a fast electron."}
    {"id":"gamma","label":"Gamma","note":"Gamma is electromagnetic radiation."}

answer: alpha

explanation:
    An alpha particle, 2 protons and 2 neutrons, is the nucleus of a helium atom.
